Referee Report
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this paper, the author sets out to examine the impact of excise taces
 on consumption and public health.
 Since excise taxes are assesed to the manufacturers of tobacco products,
 it is unclear how the taxes will impact producers and consumers, and who
 will bear the incidence of the tax.
 The author provides two explanations of how excise taxes on producers could
 affect consumers: (1) tobacco production is altered, which changes the
 type of tobacco products available in the retail market; (2) producers
 simply pass on the burden of the tax by increasing prices.
 The author claims that this paper offers the first analysis of the effect
 of federal tobacco excise taxes on tobacco produciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In 2009, the federal excise tax rate changed as a result of CHIPRA.
 So, the author measures the impact of this law change on different types
 of tobacco production, including cigarettes, loose tobacco products, cigards,
 and smokeless tobacco.
 In order to measure the effect of CHIPRA on tobacco production, the author
 estimates the counterfactual levels of tobacco production if CHIPRA was
 never enacted.
 To do this, he uses monthly data on Tobacco produciton from the U.S.
 Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau of the U.S.
 Treasury.
 The identifying assumption is that conditional on the included covariates,
 the type of tobacco production in the pre-CHIPRA period would have remained
 the same in the post-CHIPRA period.
 To estimate the counterfactual levels of production, the author exploits
 the variation in excise tax rates within states over time.
 He also estimates how much of the tax is passed on too consumers in the
 form of higher retail prices, as well as the price elasticity of taxable
 sales of cigarettes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The author finds that both producers and consumers alter their behavior
 in meaninful ways as a response to the change in the excise taxes caused
 by CHIPRA.
 Total produciton of tobacco decreased, and manfacturers shifted their productio
n to substitute goods.
 Moreover, the estimated range of the price elasticity of taxable sales
 for cigarettes is in line with previous literature, and it is relatively
 inelastic.
 Consequently, consumers bear the entire incidence of the excise taxes in
 the form of higher retail prices.
 The author goes on to claim that excise taxes do, in fact, reduce tobacco
 consumption, and this result could have a significant influence on policy
 decisions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Contribution to the Literature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While this paper offers new insight by examining the effect of excise taxes
 on tobacco production, I do not believe that the empirical results, nor
 policy implicaitons, make an incredibly important contribution to the literatur
e.
 This is because tobacco producers simply transfer their factors of production
 into substitutable goods, which was somewhat expected from the outset.
 Furthermore, the implication that incidence of the excise taxes are extensively
 transferred to consumers is not a surprising consequence.
 In fact, Evans, Ringel, and Stech (1999) presented the same result by utitlizin
g more convincing variable data and empirical methods.
 Finally, in regards to the price elasticity of taxable sales, this paper
 offers similar estimates from previous literature, even though it incorporates
 the law changes caused by CHIPRA.
 Above all, the empirical method implemented by the author, along with his
 underlying identification strategy, are somewhat questionable.
 If anything, this paper seems to be taking a structural approach as opposed
 to reduced-form.
 The main method the author implements is to estimate a counterfactual equation,
 and then compare the estimated changes to the observed changes.
 I have have seen very few reduced-form papers use this as a primary empirical
 strategy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Critical Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Impact of CHIPRA on Tobacca Production
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated previously, the author measures the effect of CHIPRA on tobacco
 production by first estimating counterfactual levels of production for
 tobacco products.
 He estimates a linear parametric model that includes month dummies, a linear
 time trend, and state-level cigarette excise rates prior to the CHIPRA
 law change.
 By estimating this equation, the author obtains the expected production
 levels of tobacco conditional on the included covariates.
 He can then compares the observed changes in the post-CHIPRA period with
 the estimated expected levels of production.
 The data he uses to estimate the counterfactual equation stretches over
 84 months, from April 2002 to March 2009.
 The esimates are then used on 36 months following the law change.
 But, the full data set runs from January 2000 through October 2013.
 Why restrict the analysis to 84 months in the pre-period, and 36 months
 in the post-period? No specific reason is given By including the additional
 data, would the estimates have changed significantly? If possible, I would
 have actually liked to see the estimation of the countefactual equation
 incorporate all of the available data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Remember, the author is identifying off of within-state variation of excise
 tax rates over time.
 However, how much variation could have actually occurred during a seven
 year period? The author does not provide any extensive overview of the
 data, nor an expository on whether there is an adequate level of variation,
 i.e., there are many tax changes.
 I suspect not enough tax changes occured in seven years to properly identify
 the estimating equation.
 It should be noted that Evans, Ringel, and Stech (1999) had ERS data for
 50 states over 30 years, so there were many tax changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, the estimating equation excludes state dummies, which is a very
 serious problem.
 In fact, the variable corresponding to the state-level cigarette excise
 rates offers no subscript to identify the specific state.
 According to the author, this variable of interest is actually the consumption
 weighted state cigarette excise tax rate in each year for all products.
 The only subscript attached to this variable denotes time.
 So, by excluding state dummies, it seems as though the author is actually
 identifying off of time series variation.
 Consequently, this implies that treatment is the only reason production
 differs systematically from pre to post.
 Obviously, this is a difficult assumption to believe since state specific
 shocks are possible.
 Finally, there is no mention of the assumptions associated with the standard
 errors, how the standard errors are computed, and what is the level of
 clustering.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The author does perform single robustness check by analyze periods that
 omit months immediately preceding and following the enactment of CHIPRA.
 But, why is there no mention of time-varying control variables associated
 with production? The author could provide an additional robustness check
 by showing how the estimates differ when including these controls.
 Moving on, how do we know consumers and producers did not anticipate the
 change in the law? Also, the change in the law took place during a recessionary
 period, which could have unobserved effects on the production of tobacco
 products.
 So, the estimated effect of excise taxes on tobacco production could be
 inconsistent.
 Instead, the effect may have been driven by producer adjustments due the
 recession.
 To address this issue, the author could have conducted a permutation test
 by utilizing all of his available data, changing the date CHIPRA was implemente
d, obtaining estimates from the placebo treatment effects, and calculating
 where the actual treatment effect lies in the empirical CDF.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Impact of Excise Taces on Cigarette Prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To measure the incidence excise taxes have on consumers in the form of higher
 prices, the author utilizes annual state-level data from 2000 to 2012.
 In the estimating equation, price is regressed on the exise tax of state
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (the key variable of interest), state fixed effects, a flexible time trend,
 and time-varying state-level characteristics.
 The key identifying assumption is that the tax rate is exogenous to the
 current retail price.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The author gives one explanation for why the identifying assumption may
 be violated, and performs a robustness check to verify his persumption
 is false.
 However, shocks to supply and demand could also cause a change in retail
 prices, and then lawmakers may respond as a result.
 For example, the time frame of the data includes the recession.
 It is quite plausible that due to the recession, more people attempted
 to quit smoking in order to save money, which caused the retail price of
 cigarettes to decrease.
 Or, because of decreased investment in capital, producers scale back their
 production lines, which also influences the retail price.
 Then, lawmakers respond to the changes in prices accordingly.
 Thus, it would be beneficial for the author to veryify that the recession
 did not have a significant impact on retail cigarette prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Also, it is very difficult to follow his analysis concerning the pass through
 of taxes to consumers.
 I would uggest expounding on this section a bit more.
 Remember, theory suggests that the pass through would be less at the national
 level than at the state level since production at the national level is
 more inelastic.
 Moreover, Evans, Ringel, and Stech (1999) find 100% pass through at the
 state level, but not at the national level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Price Elasticity of Cigarettes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, the author estimates the price elasticity of taxable cigarette
 sales.
 Once again, it would be quite helpful for the author to provide a brief
 explanation concerning the price elasticity of taxable sales of cigarettes
 To estimate the given equation, he includes the log price of cigarettes
 in state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (the key parameter of interest), state fixed effects, a flexible time trend,
 and time-varying state-level characteristics.
 For instrunmental variables, he uses two separate specifications: (1) the
 combined state plus federal tax rate; (2) each of the terms separately.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While taxes have been utilized as instrumental variables for prices in a
 number of empirical papers, I would suggest including the standard robustness
 checks and expository associated with IV estimation.
 Show the first stage, demonstrating that the instruments are relevant and
 not subject to the problems of weak instruments.
 Also, include the reduced-form IV estimation.
 Finally, explain why the IV estimates differ from the OLS estimates.
\end_layout

